      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# How a release of Twilite is published\n\nThis is the process behind every release note, drawn as a workflow: **nine stages from reviewed changes to a published release.** It is made from the Repeatable Workflow template, so each stage is a Step or a Gate, and the order is the `workflow.next` edges between them.\n\n**Who does what.** An agent can do the reading, the drafting and the checking. The owner makes three decisions: which entries are approved (stage 03), that the release is ready to publish (stage 06), and publishing the site itself. Nothing here gives an agent those decisions, and nothing starts on a timer, a commit or an approved entry.\n\n**What never happens.** A release is never published because an entry was approved. Private review notes and source commits never go into a public release. A published release is never edited or deleted: a mistake is corrected by a new release. An approval covers the release exactly as it was shown, and if the release changes, the approval is void.\n\n**Read it by following the gold edges** from stage 01 to stage 09. Gates are the gold-tinted cards, and the three owner decisions are marked on them.\n\n**Try this:** run the Preflight Method the template hands on. It returns the nine stages in order with no errors. What it returns is a plan, not executed actions.\n\n**The result:** every release that has been published is in the release notes, `tlz://twilite.zone/products/twilite/release-notes/root.node`. The template this is made from is `tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/repeatable-workflow/root.node`."
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "manually-shaped",
          "source": "manual",
          "updatedAt": 1795557694718,
          "reason": "node-drag"
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "twilite-release-notes-workflow-root"
        }
      }
    },
